    "body": "Fixes #108304.\n\n**The Issue:**\nUsing advanced indexing (e.g., list indices like `x[[0, 1]]`) raises a `TypeError` when executed inside `tf.function` (Graph mode) or XLA contexts (like `tf.case` trace). This logic works correctly in Eager execution, creating an inconsistency between Eager and Compiled modes.\n\nThe error arises because `tensor.__getitem__` (overridden via `_slice_helper` in `tensor_getitem_override.py`) enforces strict validation that rejects list indices for Symbolic Tensors, whereas Eager Tensors handle them via `_numpy_style_getitem`.\n\n**The Fix:**\nModified `_slice_helper` in `tensorflow/python/ops/tensor_getitem_override.py` to detect advanced indexing patterns (lists, tuples, or non-scalar tensors). If detected, it explicitly falls back to `tensor._numpy_style_getitem`, which supports advanced indexing semantics (including `tf.gather` logic).\n\n**Verification:**\n- Verified using a reproduction script (monkey-patched due to build constraints) that exhibited the crash.\n- Confirmed that list indexing inside `tf.case` now succeeds in both XLA (`jit_compile=True`) and standard Graph modes.\n\n**Impact:**\nImproves parity between Eager and XLA/Graph execution, allowing valid NumPy-style indexing code to be compiled without errors.",
