    "body": "Fixes #98002.\n\nThis PR prevents segmentation faults (EXC_BAD_ACCESS) in the TFLite Eigen backend when input and output buffers alias.\n\n**The Issue:**\nThe `GemmImplUsingEigen::Run` implementation (used for non-quantized fallback execution) utilizes the `.noalias()` optimization for matrix multiplication assignments. This instructs Eigen to write directly to the destination buffer without checking for overlaps or creating temporary buffers.\nIf specific TFLite graphs or client usages (like the reported `Blosc2-Btune` case) provide aliasing buffers (where `dst` overlaps with `lhs` or `rhs`), this results in Undefined Behavior. In practice, this often leads to data corruption or segmentation faults due to invalid memory access patterns in the Eigen contraction kernel.\n\n**The Fix:**\nI have removed the `.noalias()` calls from `tensorflow/lite/kernels/cpu_backend_gemm_eigen.cc`.\nThis restores standard safe behavior: Eigen will now verify at runtime if the pointers alias. If they do, it will automatically allocate a temporary buffer for the result before copying it to the destination. If they do not, the performance overhead of the check is negligible.\n\n**Verification:**\n- **Local TFLite Interpreter Tests:** Verified that TFLite models executed via `tf.lite.Interpreter` continue to function correctly with this change.\n- **Safety:** The fix relies on the correctness of the Eigen library's aliasing detection mechanism, which is the standard safe default.\n\n**Platforms Affected:**\n- macOS (as reported in Issue #98002)\n- Any platform using the Eigen backend for TFLite (e.g., x86 without RUY, or specific fallback paths).\n",
